# How to cite Wavebit. GitHub reads this file for "Cite this repository", and Zenodo reads it
# when it archives a GitHub release. `version` and `date-released` name the release this
# commit belongs to; a release changes them together with `version` in pyproject.toml, and a
# test keeps the two versions equal.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Wavebit in your work, please cite the version you used."
type: software
title: "Wavebit: Differentiable Electromagnetic Simulation Suite"
abstract: >-
  Wavebit computes how light scatters from periodic nanostructures with rigorous
  coupled-wave analysis (RCWA) and how electromagnetic fields evolve in time with
  the finite-difference time-domain method (FDTD), behind one API on NumPy, JAX
  and PyTorch. It differentiates through the RCWA calculation, so the model that
  predicts a device's response is the model that optimizes it.
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Yongha
    affiliation: FieldAlgo
version: 0.0.4
date-released: 2026-10-17
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/fieldalgo/wavebit"
keywords:
  - electromagnetics
  - RCWA
  - FDTD
  - nanophotonics
  - automatic differentiation
  - JAX
  - PyTorch
